hi  this is dr gregory sadler i'm a  professor of philosophy  and the president and founder of an  educational consulting company called  reason i o  where we put philosophy into practice  i've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years  and i find that many people run into  difficulties reading classic  philosophical texts  sometimes it's the way things are said  or how the text is structured  but the concepts themselves are not  always that complicated and that's where  i come in  to help students and lifelong learners  i've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube  many viewers say they find them useful  what you're currently watching is part  of a new series of shorter videos  each of them focused on one core concept  from an important philosophical text  i hope you find it useful as well  another one of the social virtues which  aristotle talks about is what he calls  friendliness  and he wants to distinguish this from  friendship  per se philia and  sometimes aristotle is a little  inconsistent in his greek terminology  from from the nickel mccain ethics the  udamian ethnics to the politics to the  to the rhetoric but but in general he  distinguishes between  friendship and this this condition of  friendliness and then the emotion  involved in being friendly towards  somebody  and this is a state of character  and it's a mean position meaning that  it is in between two vices  there's a vice of excess and divisive  deficiency  too much too little and  what constitutes being a friendly  or a medical or  somebody with good social skills type of  person  for aristotle it really has to do with  giving pleasure and pain to other people  and in general if we have the chance to  give them pleasure by engaging  in social activities with them aristotle  thinks we ought to do that  that there's something wrong with a  person who doesn't want to do that or  sort of perversely wants to give pain to  other people  now should we go along with everything  no  so the person who's friendly they go  along with they join in  they approve the right things  they don't approve everything  as a matter of fact they will have  certain limits where they will refuse or  criticize  the wrong things so if i suggest hey  let's go shoplifting  a person who's being friendly doesn't  say to me yeah that's a great idea  they say i think it's a bad idea let's  not do that  and then if i keep pushing they'll  eventually say hey no i'm not i'm not  going along with you as a matter of fact  i don't think i want to  talk to you after this that would be an  example of what aristotle  now on the other hand if i invite  somebody to a party and it's you know  a normal kind of party it's not you know  something really weird  or off the wall like uh you know  say a snuff party or something like that  then  they say yeah let's let's let's go let  me check my calendar let me see if i can  make this  they won't refuse to engage in things  now if you say  hey i want to go to this rave and while  people are  dancing around i want to punch a lot of  people in the face then they're going to  say  there's something wrong with you that's  not what you should do at a party  you know we could make up all sorts of  examples like this  and so it has to do with what sort of  activities you're willing to  to engage in are they are they the right  kind of activities or the wrong kind of  activities and somebody might say  oh well that's all very subjective this  is a common  objection that's always made and it is  difficult sometimes to determine exactly  where the boundary lines  uh fall but if you're looking at things  from an aristotelian perspective  that's not the case it's not all up for  grabs it's not all just a matter of  opinion  there are some considerations that we  ought to be guided by  honor and usefulness or the opposite of  these  dishonor shamefulness and  harm if something is going to actually  be harmful  to a friend whether in the short term  or in the long term if it's damaging to  them  then you're not actually being a friend  by saying yeah yeah man i think you  ought to do that that's a great idea  you're probably setting them up for for  harm and that's being a bad friend  that's actually being over here on this  side on the other hand if you know if  your friend is suggesting something  that's good for them even if it's not  something that you perhaps  want to do like let's say they decide  you have a friend that you're always  going out to the bars with and they say  you know i gotta curtail this activity  it's costing me too much money and it's  taking a toll on my  health and i'm not getting the work done  or the studying done that i want to do  i'm going to cut back on that then the  friendly thing to do is to say  yeah that's a good idea you should  curtail that maybe i'll actually join in  with you on that  um helping a friend quit smoking for  example would be something along those  lines  um it's something that's good for them  good in the sense of  of useful not not just in the sense of  pure utility but it's useful in the  sense that it's beneficial for them  then at a higher level we have what's  honorable and dishonorable  and for for aristotelian virtue ethics  this is something  very important and this is at a higher  level and again you can say well isn't  that purely subjective no for aristotle  it's not  there are certain guidelines for this he  provides you with these and in many of  his  his works you can you can read through  them  and you can agree or disagree with them  but before you worry about whether it's  subjective  read the aristotle so  this person is going to observe certain  certain limits  and they're going to observe these  limits both for themselves and for  others the others who they're involved  within social interaction  now let's look at one of the extremes so  the person who goes along with or  approves  everything whether it's right or wrong  they seem like they're actually friendly  that  that often is what trips people up  there's this sense that  if we're friends with somebody we should  love them  you know respect them hang out with them  no matter what that's what it means to  be friends or  or you know comrades or bros or pick  whatever you like you know  i mean side note i tend to think that  people use the word broader  are rather dull  and they often end up over here there's  something about  the culture of that but there's all  sorts of other things along those lines  too  if you get in with a bad group of people  who have bad  characters probably this is what they  want in a friend because they don't want  to be told  you're doing the wrong thing there's a  lot of people who cannot handle  criticism  uh in part because they are not actually  happy with who they are and sort of  things that they're doing because they  know in their heart that  what they're doing is is either wrong or  harmful or screwed up or  counterproductive  and they don't really want to hear that  they want to lose themselves in the  activities that they're doing and the  pleasures that they get from them  rather than developing morally so you're  not actually  helping somebody out by going along with  them doing wrong things  people like this are unwilling to stand  up to  other people and say no i'm not going to  go along with it what you're doing is  is actually bad or harmful or wrong  because they're  unwilling to give other people pain and  this could be that's where aristotle  stops we could do an analysis and go  much further  why are they unwilling to give other  people pain perhaps because of you know  traumatic childhood where when they gave  when they actually stood up to people  they were beaten down it could be a  desperate need to be liked by other  people  regardless of what your childhood is  like some people are just you know  desperately needy that way it could be  just a general lack of character  um there's quite a few people i think  who are are willing to go along  fairly easily with wrong things provided  that their group is doing it  provided that it's it's couched in the  wrong they're the right way  you know and they're not faced with uh  direct responsibility  themselves now somebody who goes along  with these sorts of things  aerosol has two different terms for this  he calls it obsequiousness  and flattering and why does he  make a distinction there it depends on  whether you're just doing this because  that's the way you are  and you're not getting anything out of  it in which case you're you're obsessed  or are you trying to get something out  of it are you kissing up  to somebody else and telling them that  they're a good person  you know like for example you belong to  the entourage of somebody who's a jerk  that you're a flatterer a colocase  a greek  term that was you know sort of universal  reprobation  call somebody that there are some people  who are just that way even if they don't  get anything out of it  oftentimes they get taken advantage of  themselves and that's being obsequious  that's  that's a certain lack of self-esteem  that's involved there  it takes self-esteem to actually be able  to tell other people no here's where the  limit is  you're crossing the line here now on the  other hand there's people who  have this this vice deficiency that he  calls  unfriendliness or quarrelsomeness and  they're sort of the mirror  opposite of this vice over here they  disapprove of  everything or most things even right  things  they are unwilling to give other people  pleasure they're unwilling to join in  they are the contrarians for  the sake of being contrarians these are  the people who are i  you know i have to admit that personally  i can't stand it when people say i just  want to play devil's advocate because  usually  you know what that means is i want to  indulge my my  vicious character of being a contrarian  and raising all sorts of problems just  for the sake of doing so  um sometimes you know you know there are  actual devil's advocates who would  belong over here but  more often over here  now why would a person have that sort of  character  in a way to give other people pleasure  to say that their  right is to you know  indulge them to engage them to withhold  that is to  in some respects feel like one has some  sort of power  over them it's a loser completely  illusory power it doesn't actually  contribute to anything  but i think that there are quite a few  people who do that  so we have one vice over here  deficiency people who are  too quick to judge too quick to say no  they do so on the wrong occasions  they draw lines too arbitrarily too  quickly  with the wrong people they disapprove of  even the  right things over here we have the  person who doesn't observe  limits other than to not have limits you  know these are the ones who will say  hey man you shouldn't judge  and which is a judgment right it's being  judgmental about other people  and that usually stems from a fear or  unwillingness to actually take  responsibility as a human being for  engaging in judgment and right here we  have the person who  is actually in the mean position who  has some standards who follows through  on those standards perhaps not  completely consistently depending on  what degree of virtue they develop  but actually is willing to say no to  some things  and to which frees you up by the way to  say  yes all the more exuberantly or  enthusiastically to the right things  and on that point i will end i just want  to  reiterate that one more time that's a  plagiarism there to say reiterate one  more time  by saying no to the wrong things by not  being this person  it frees you up to say yes  in a more intense more  enthusiastic more rewarding way  to the right things

